Follow WCAG 2.2 Level AA principles wherever applicable and supported by the framework.
Do not claim formal accessibility certification unless a qualified assessment has actually taken place.
A. Colour contrast
Use the required colour palette:
Deep forest green: #093017
Primary green: #2CA757
Light green: #74D885
Pale mint: #D3FCDA
Use these colours thoughtfully and preserve sufficient contrast.
The deep forest green should be the main text and navigation colour where appropriate.
The pale mint should be used for light backgrounds and subtle surfaces.
The primary green may be used for primary actions when the text and surrounding contrast remain accessible.
The light green should primarily be used for accents or larger visual areas where sufficient contrast can be maintained.
Do not place small white text on a light green background if the contrast is insufficient.
Do not place small light-green text on pale mint.
Check contrast ratios for normal text, large text, buttons, form controls, charts and important graphical elements.
Aim for at least 4.5:1 contrast for normal text and 3:1 for large text and relevant interface components.
If a requested colour combination fails contrast, adjust the foreground/background combination while keeping the overall palette recognisable.
